$$
u(x,t)=\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} F(x-y,t) f(y) dy
$$
where $f(y)$ is a test function satisfying $f(0)=0$ and $f^\prime(0)=1$. Define $\psi(x,t)=e^{-(t/5)}$. Then we can calculate the integral and write:
$$
u(x,t)=\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \psi(x-y,t) f(y) dy
$$
Let $u_t(x,t)$ be the derivative of $u(x,t)$ with respect to time. Then $u_t(x,t)$ can be calculated as follows:
$$
u_t(x,t)=\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \psi_t(x-y,t) f(y) dy
$$
Where $\psi_t(x,y,t)=e^{-(t/5)} \psi_x(x-y,t)$. We can now use this information to calculate the derivative of $u(x,t)$ with respect to time:
$$
u_t(x,t)=e^{-(t/5)} \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \psi_x(x-y,t) f(y) dy-e^{-(t/5)} \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \psi_t(x-y,t) f(y) dy
$$
